Difference Between User-Defined Variables and Environment Variables in Shell

🟢 1. User-Defined Variables

These are the variables that you create inside your script or terminal session.
They exist only within that shell (and not for other programs).

#!/bin/bash

name="Aayushi"
echo "Hello, $name!"

📌 Explanation:
	•	name is created by you.
	•	It exists only in this shell (temporary).
	•	If you open a new terminal, $name won’t exist there.

✅ Use Case:

To store temporary values while running a script — like usernames, file paths, counters, etc.


These are your own storage boxes inside the script.
They exist only while your script is running.

LOG_FILE="/tmp/aws_env_check.log"
CURRENT_DATE=$(date +"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S")

Here:
	•	LOG_FILE = where you’ll save results.
	•	CURRENT_DATE = current date and time.

➡️ These two are local. Once your script ends, they disappear.



🔵 2. Environment Variables

These are system-wide variables that affect the behavior of the shell and other programs.
They are inherited by child processes (subshells)

export PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin
echo $PATH

📌 Explanation:
	•	PATH is an environment variable used by Linux to find executables.
	•	When you export a variable, it becomes available to child shells (like when a script is run or another command is executed).

These are global boxes that the system already knows about — like your name, home folder, or cloud setup.
They are visible to every script and command.

AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
AWS_DEFAULT_REGION

🔍 3. What the script does
	1.	It prints a message saying:
👉 “Hey, I’m checking your AWS setup…”
	2.	Then it looks at those environment variables.
	•	If they’re empty → ❌ it says “Credentials not set!”
	•	If they’re filled → ✅ it says “Credentials configured.”
	3.	If your region is not set, it automatically adds one:


export AWS_DEFAULT_REGION="us-east-1"

This means:
➡️ “If you didn’t tell me your region, I’ll use this default and share it with other tools.”

	4.	Finally, it saves the output to a log file at:

/tmp/aws_env_check.log
